"AArch64 pair-atomic + FEAT_LSE2"

(* Uses herd7's bundled aarch64.cat by `include' only and contains none of
   its text; this file's own content is ours. *)

(*
 * cats/aarch64-pair.cat with ONE change: the write side of both pair axioms is
 * widened from `pairRMWW' (the two writes of a read-modify-write) to `pairW'
 * (the two writes of ANY multi-event instruction).
 *
 * That is exactly the change cats/aarch64-pair.cat declines to make, and says
 * so in terms:
 *
 *     `same-instr' is a built-in ... relating *all* events of any multi-event
 *     instruction -- including the two reads of an LDP and the two writes of
 *     an STP.  Constraining both sides with it would forbid LDP/STP tearing,
 *     i.e. impose FEAT_LSE2 on baseline hardware.  That would be wrong.
 *
 * The CoreLang target accepts requiring FEAT_LSE2 (Armv8.4),
 * so for the CoreLang target baseline it is no longer wrong -- it is the
 * point.  This file is the reference model for the target's tier-2 access.
 *
 * VALIDATION
 *
 * Measured when this file was added, against cats/aarch64-pair.cat and against
 * stock aarch64.cat, all under `-variant mixed'.  litmus/aarch64-lse2/ holds
 * the four probes and they run on every ./check-herd.sh.
 *
 *   probe               stock        pair.cat     THIS FILE
 *   I-ldp-stp           Sometimes 4  Sometimes 4  Never 2
 *   I-ldp-stp-swapped   Sometimes 4  Sometimes 4  Never 2
 *   K-ldp-stp-sep       Sometimes 4  Sometimes 4  Never 3
 *   J-plain-str-tear    Sometimes 4  Sometimes 4  Sometimes 4   \/- control
 *
 * The state set is the evidence, not the count.  Under this model I-ldp-stp
 * leaves exactly
 *
 *   1:X4=0; 1:X5=0;
 *   1:X4=1; 1:X5=1;
 *
 * so the observer sees the STP entirely or not at all.  That is single-copy
 * atomicity itself rather than a model that merely forbids more.
 *
 * Three things that make the table worth believing:
 *
 * - stock and pair.cat agree on all four probes, so this is a real change
 *   rather than a re-confirmation of something already in force.  pair.cat
 *   does stop exactly one token short of FEAT_LSE2, as its comment claims.
 * - both tearing orientations flip together.  An earlier version of this
 *   development was found forbidding a pair probe in one word order and allowing it
 *   swapped; I-ldp-stp-swapped is what establishes that it does not recur
 *   here, rather than an argument that it would not.
 * - the J control keeps all four states, including both torn ones, so two
 *   separate STRs still tear and the axiom does not over-reach.  That is also
 *   right against real hardware: single-copy atomicity of the *load* still
 *   lets it land between two independent stores.
 *
 * Conservativity, verdict and state count, bit-identical in every case:
 *
 *   litmus/aarch64-pair/  (9 tests)   pair.cat vs this file
 *   litmus/aarch64/      (25 tests)   pair.cat vs this file
 *   litmus/aarch64/      (25 tests)   bare aarch64.cat vs this file
 *
 * The CoreLang target's tier-2 access is what it is for.
 *)

catdep

include "aarch64.cat"

(* Any two events of one instruction. *)
let pairAny = same-instr

(* The two WRITES of one multi-event instruction -- STP as well as CASP.
   cats/aarch64-pair.cat restricts this to range(rmw); that restriction is
   precisely what this file removes. *)
let pairW = same-instr & (W * W)

irreflexive (pairAny ; fr ; pairW ; rf) as pair-atomic

(* A multi-event instruction's writes are one single-copy-atomic access.
   `sca2' widens aarch64.cat's `sca-class' by them; `ob2' is aarch64.cat's
   ordered-before, referenced by name (`hw-reqs', `obs', `Exp-obs', `Tag-obs'),
   with the explicit and tag observations also allowed to continue by an
   `sca2' step.  As sca-class <= sca2, this is the old obs2/ob2 pair, with `+'
   for the recursive `ob2 ; ob2'. *)
let sca2 = (sca-class | pairW)+

let ob2 = (hw-reqs | obs | Exp-obs ; sca2 | Tag-obs ; sca2)+

irreflexive ob2 as pair-external
